1785467561961883	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwAQ		item.create	bn-2eg	{"description":"The submission key is dropped when it rides along in the same write as the text.\n\nReproduced against codex (v0.146.0) in a 200x50 vessel agent:\n\n    vessel spawn --name probe --rows 50 --cols 200 --cwd DIR -- codex\n    vessel send probe 'Reply with exactly ENTER_TEST.' --enter\n    # 6s later: text sits in the composer, unsubmitted\n\n    vessel send-keys probe enter\n    # submits immediately, agent answers\n\n--newline (LF) fails identically to --enter (CR), so this is not key encoding.\nThe distinguishing factor is that the key arrives in the same write as the text:\ndelivering it as a separate call, after a short pause, works every time.\n\nLikely cause: the TUI applies timing-based paste detection, and a trailing\nCR/LF inside what looks like one pasted burst is treated as content rather than\nas a keypress. Same reasoning as bracketed paste.\n\nImpact: --enter is the documented ergonomic path for driving an interactive\nagent, and it silently does nothing for the main class of program vessel exists\nto drive. The failure is silent -- the prompt looks delivered.\n\nPossible fixes: flush the text and delay a beat before the key, send the key as\na separate PTY write, or document that --enter is unsafe for TUIs and point at\nsend-keys.","kind":"bug","size":"s","title":"send --enter and --newline do not submit to TUI agents"}	blake3:wJ8ZE23_xB1IECVvTcsJegUJgW0ESgMx7nxxtcjO-qQ
1785467572380728	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwAg		item.create	bn-1a0f	{"description":"Sending a multi-line prompt to a TUI agent currently requires hand-rolling\nbracketed paste through send-bytes:\n\n    1b5b3230307e + <utf-8 text as hex> + 1b5b3230317e     # ESC[200~ .. ESC[201~\n\nWithout it, the first newline submits a truncated prompt and every remaining\nline lands as its own turn -- three separate agent turns for one prompt, each\nbilled, none of them what was asked.\n\nVerified working: wrapped that way, a three-line prompt lands in codex's\ncomposer as three lines, unsubmitted, and then submits as a single turn.\n\nProposal: 'vessel send ID --paste TEXT' (or --bracketed) that wraps the payload\nin the paste markers. Reading the text from stdin with '-' would help too;\nprompts for coding agents are routinely long enough to be awkward as argv.\n\nThis is the single most common thing an orchestrator needs and the most\nerror-prone to reproduce by hand, since the failure is silent and looks like the\nagent misunderstood the prompt.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Add bracketed-paste mode to send, for multi-line prompts"}	blake3:C2RP45CX5tvoAyQnKAM-_LNZwvnY1e5MRg1_kf0W-IY
1785468342965060	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyLQ		item.move	bn-2eg	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:SqC6TSbyuaMTVW_NaC86rYmbeSHvxHbYSuH1gqCAzVQ
1785468342969968	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyLg		item.assign	bn-2eg	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:YCQXv-Om4VThwumPInbS5UaNoY3trF-bIRy3kfKmndY
1785469572162988	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyLw		item.comment	bn-2eg	{"body":"Root cause confirmed and fixed in ws bn-2eg.\n\nTwo distinct defects, both in the Send/SendBytes PTY write path:\n\n1. Submit key rode in the same write(2) as the text (the reported bug). Now the\n   text is flushed first, then a configurable pause (DEFAULT_SUBMIT_DELAY_MS =\n   50), then the CR/LF as its own write. New --submit-delay-ms tunes it; 0 opts\n   out for shells.\n\n2. The PTY master is O_NONBLOCK (pty.rs:216) but the handler matched\n   nix::unistd::write(..) as Ok(_), discarding short-write counts. Any payload\n   past the PTY input buffer was silently truncated. Added write_all_pty() with\n   short-write + EAGAIN retry and a 5s cap.\n\nAlso had to restructure the handler: the retry loop cannot run while holding\nthe manager lock, because pty_reader_task needs that same lock to drain output.\nA child that blocks writing its echo then never reads the rest of the payload.\nVerified: with the lock held, a 156 KB send fails 'PTY accepted 26112 of 156000\nbytes'. Now the fd is dup(2)'d and the manager lock released before writing,\nwith a new per-agent Agent::write_lock preserving write atomicity.\n\nRegression tests added (both verified to fail without the fix)."}	blake3:k5_fpkkHTCCpB-458DsgW3qB0TL7CyE0tGF_ncsXqWU
1785472491045969	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyMA		item.move	bn-2eg	{"state":"done"}	blake3:MkWANv6qYfRrvBQXVgmXv_665fuAS66jr75tHIi2Jhw
1785472491064232	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyMQ		item.move	bn-1a0f	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:ZSxCf_Zz5ZDsFhGrzixx7KQF-X-nBJoiPFigW0Acxs0
1785472491067404	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyMg		item.assign	bn-1a0f	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:NITXtILbBZtc_sseaexcXqQRLcrfnVEbzGe0NCuu7uA
1785473391251130	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyMw		item.comment	bn-1a0f	{"body":"Implemented in ws bn-1a0f (merged).\n\n- 'vessel send ID --paste TEXT' (-p, alias --bracketed) wraps in ESC[200~..ESC[201~\n- '-' as TEXT reads the payload from stdin\n- --paste --enter pastes then submits as one turn (uses the bn-2eg separate-write\n  submit key, so the CR lands outside the envelope)\n\nEmbedded ESC[201~ in the text is stripped rather than forwarded: it would close\nthe bracket early and deliver the remainder as live keystrokes, so a prompt that\nquotes the sequence could submit itself partway through. Terminals filter it for\nthe same reason. Covered by unit tests in src/server/mod.rs.\n\nCoverage: 5 unit tests (wrapping, empty payload, terminator stripping, adjacent\nterminators, introducer passthrough), 2 PTY tests in tests/send_pty.rs, 1 CLI\ntest for the stdin path."}	blake3:PAbEiWxcLzFDdyetlv9n9xGN-mAXS8jzajKzDYfrrmI
1785473391267470	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyNA		item.move	bn-1a0f	{"state":"done"}	blake3:isf7uroqaeJtyZlUUvs_7rFSmKAPKiQzC-bKOr_aXEM
1785473400779599	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyNQ		item.create	bn-20v1	{"description":"cargo test --workspace intermittently fails or hangs on a 64-core machine. Reproduced on unmodified trunk (ac4748c), so this predates the bn-2eg/bn-1a0f work.\n\nObserved failures, each in a different run:\n  - test_transcript_tail FAILED\n  - test_oversized_frame_rejected_without_dispatch hung >60s\n  - pty::tests::test_spawn_echo hung >60s\n\nPasses reliably with 'cargo test --test integration -- --test-threads=4'.\n\nLikely cause: every async_test! stands up its own runtime sized to nproc, so N\nconcurrent tests means N x 64 workers. Tests that run for seconds rather than\nmilliseconds widen the window where all of them are live at once and push peak\nthread count over a cliff. This is why tests/send_pty.rs was split into its own\nbinary in bn-2eg -- cargo runs test binaries one at a time, which keeps the peak\ndown.\n\nWorth fixing since CI runs plain 'cargo test --workspace'. Options: cap the\nper-test runtime worker count, or set a test-threads default in the harness.","kind":"bug","labels":["tracking"],"title":"Test suite is flaky under high parallelism (64-core box)"}	blake3:AHVll4nzD8KWPdcpjGR4ORj67pQCFazjxcjzmcvLwYc
1785474246919955	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyNg		item.create	bn-1dxu	{"description":"vessel send takes exactly one agent ID. There is no way to deliver the same\ninput to a group, even though the selector pattern is already established\nelsewhere in the CLI.\n\nCurrent state:\n  - Send, SendBytes, SendKeys all take a required positional 'id: String'\n    (src/cli.rs:291, :332, :352). Request::Send is likewise 'id: String', and\n    the server handler resolves a single mgr.get_mut(&id).\n  - kill (src/cli.rs:222) and signal (:256) already accept --label, --all and\n    --proc with an optional ID.\n  - list (:203), subscribe and events accept --label as a filter.\n\nSo the send family is the odd one out.\n\nWorkaround today is a client-side loop:\n\n    for id in $(vessel list --label worker --format json | jq -r '.agents[].id'); do\n        vessel send \"$id\" \"$PROMPT\" --paste --enter\n    done\n\nThat is N round trips, and since bn-2eg each --enter costs ~50ms of submit\ndelay, paid serially.\n\nTwo design questions the kill/signal precedent does NOT settle:\n\n1. Partial failure. kill is idempotent and tolerates no-match, so it can report\n   success loosely. A broadcast send that writes to three agents and hits EIO on\n   the fourth must report which agents actually took the input. Returning a bare\n   Ok would reintroduce exactly the 'looks delivered, wasn't' failure mode that\n   bn-2eg existed to fix. Probably needs a per-agent result list in the response\n   rather than Response::Ok, which is a protocol change.\n\n2. Concurrency. Each write holds that agent's Agent::write_lock and may sleep\n   for the submit delay (bn-2eg). Fanning out concurrently is right so the\n   per-agent delays overlap instead of stacking -- but the manager lock must be\n   released before the writes, as it already is, and the fan-out must not\n   serialize on it.\n\nScope: src/cli.rs, src/protocol.rs (Request::Send selector fields + a response\nshape that can carry per-agent outcomes), src/server/mod.rs, src/main.rs, tests.\n\nWorth deciding whether send-bytes and send-keys get the same selectors at the\nsame time, for consistency.\n\nContext: raised while finishing bn-2eg and bn-1a0f.","kind":"task","title":"send: target agents by --label / --all / --proc"}	blake3:DcvQ7AwFK4P7sqD2IyjGmRlAT3j692R2BazMvzBxqYA
1785475537782844	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyNw		item.move	bn-1dxu	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:-qU9ag2L3ue7gpHeVMPBVaIyyleDKsDt2GTLfpYX1AY
1785475537787704	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyOA		item.assign	bn-1dxu	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:f1wvNWGA-IhgPW7xXqQwpqM_1yz6Ctbu_2SjEDdDMO0
1785476794107878	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyOQ		item.comment	bn-1dxu	{"body":"Implemented and merged.\n\nSelectors --label / --proc / --all now work on send, send-bytes and send-keys.\n\nBoth design questions from the bone resolved:\n\n1. Partial failure. New Response::SendResults carries one SendOutcome per\n   matched agent; the CLI prints a line each and exits non-zero if any missed\n   the input. Requests naming a single ID keep the Ok/Error contract, so\n   existing callers (including the exec path and testing.rs) are untouched.\n\n2. Concurrency. One task per agent, each taking only that agent's write_lock,\n   so per-agent submit delays overlap rather than stack and no task holds two\n   locks.\n\nAlso: selector resolution is now one helper shared with kill, so the two cannot\ndrift. --all keeps precedence over the filters rather than intersecting, which\nis kill's long-standing behaviour and is preserved deliberately.\n\nPositional shift: with a selector the agent ID is omitted, so clap parks the\npayload in the ID slot and it is shifted back (split_send_positionals /\nsplit_send_keys_positionals). ID + selector together is rejected, not guessed.\n\nFlag note: --proc has no -p short form on the send family, because -p is --paste\non send. kill and signal keep -p for --proc. Worth revisiting if the asymmetry\nbites.\n\nCoverage: 8 unit tests for the positional split, 4 PTY fan-out tests, 4 CLI\ntests (fan-out, no-match exits non-zero, ID+selector rejected, send-keys\nvariadic shift)."}	blake3:bRG1jRnTC6lnHIy1_3qoCdya7iX53mK0HsIRP8vB4sY
1785476794123926	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyOg		item.move	bn-1dxu	{"state":"done"}	blake3:oWDLIngTqgtkKUmbU-B4M-E4W558UQMnNWPKElPJdQI
1785512760548830	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyOw		item.comment	bn-20v1	{"body":"More evidence from the bn-1a0f / bn-1dxu work.\n\nReproduced twice more, both times as 'pty::tests::test_spawn_echo has been\nrunning for over 60 seconds', each with a clean pass on the immediate re-run of\nthe identical code. Same test, same commit, different outcome -- so this is not\ntest-specific logic, it is peak concurrency.\n\nIt also hit on the merged-trunk verification for both bn-2eg and bn-1dxu, which\nmeans anyone running the documented 'just test' gate sees it intermittently, not\njust CI.\n\nRoot cause confirmed in the runtime source rather than inferred:\nasupersync's RuntimeConfig::default_worker_threads() is\nstd::thread::available_parallelism() (config.rs:282). Every async_test! builds\nits own runtime via runtime::task::block_on, and cargo runs tests within a\nbinary in parallel at one thread per core. On this box that is up to 64 tests x\n64 workers. RuntimeBuilder::worker_threads(n) (builder.rs:1402) exists to cap\nit, and block_on is only ever called by async_test! -- production main builds\nits own runtime directly -- so capping there cannot affect the shipped binary.\n\nThe tokio backend is unaffected: #[tokio::test] defaults to a current-thread\nruntime."}	blake3:St1-Kqh5oaiXYeSr9o6hsaCZRiiXTEUSeIUqNTg77Zs
1785512768123467	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyPA		item.update	bn-20v1	{"field":"urgency","value":"urgent"}	blake3:qUGGMdl7KBn1Fmv1wpYGnzjuBIO3pX8bIY9k85BOgEM
1785512773137137	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyPQ		item.move	bn-20v1	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:QASDG583gw0D7ZlywrXBsj-GzoC0forg2i1B6s5WjkM
1785512773140493	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyPg		item.assign	bn-20v1	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:Xs4C3pDfX0mPkjK-E97InxsGTdpte58gFlEDtUWDuZc
1785512917263692	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyPw		item.create	bn-3dsg	{"description":"The default runtime backend (runtime-asupersync) pins asupersync 0.2.9;\n0.3.10 is published. A 0.x minor bump is semver-breaking, so this needs a\nreal compile-and-test pass, not a version bump.\n\nInitial survey (source of both versions compared locally):\n  - Both features vessel enables still exist in 0.3.10: test-internals,\n    proc-macros.\n  - Every module vessel touches is still present: net, io, sync, time,\n    signal, runtime, channel, and the Cx re-export.\n  - The feature set changed a lot around the edges (0.3.10 adds atp-cli,\n    atpd-daemon, benchmark-adapters, wasm-runtime, and more), but none of\n    that is on vessel's path.\n\nSo it may be close to mechanical. Unverified until it compiles.\n\nSurface vessel depends on, i.e. what to check:\n  - asupersync::net::{UnixListener, UnixStream, UnixOwnedReadHalf,\n    UnixOwnedWriteHalf} and bind_unix_listener\n  - asupersync::io::{AsyncReadExt, AsyncWriteExt, BufReader, AsyncBufRead,\n    read_line, ReadLine}\n  - asupersync::sync::Mutex (wrapped in runtime.rs to hide the Cx argument)\n  - asupersync::channel::broadcast\n  - asupersync::time::Sleep\n  - asupersync::signal::{Signal, SignalKind, signal}\n  - asupersync::runtime::{RuntimeBuilder, RuntimeHandle, JoinHandle}\n  - asupersync::Cx and Cx::current()\n\nWatch for: RuntimeBuilder::worker_threads, which bn-20v1 now depends on to\ncap the per-test runtime; and the Mutex API, since runtime.rs wraps it to\npaper over the Cx-threaded signature. If 0.3.10 gained an owned-guard\nMutex (lock_owned), the fan-out in bn-1dxu could drop its Arc juggling.\n\nVerify against both backends -- Cargo.toml also carries runtime-tokio, and\nthe two are mutually exclusive, so 'cargo clippy --workspace' only covers\nthe default.","kind":"task","title":"Upgrade asupersync 0.2.9 -> 0.3.10"}	blake3:vWLLDeNvKMJ1j0Zdx6pkDV_kI2eo_5RrrVfVHuqa-K0
1785515277588309	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyQA		item.comment	bn-20v1	{"body":"Correction: my earlier diagnosis in this bone was wrong.\n\nI attributed the flakiness to asupersync runtime worker threads\n(available_parallelism per async_test!) and proposed capping them. I implemented\nthat cap and A/B tested it against the same binary via an env override:\n\n  64 workers (pre-fix behaviour): 3/5 full-suite runs failed\n   4 workers (the 'fix'):         3/5 full-suite runs failed\n\nNo effect. The tell I had missed: pty::tests::test_spawn_echo is a plain\nsynchronous #[test] in the lib test binary. It never touches the async runtime,\nso no runtime setting could ever have affected it. I reverted the cap.\n\nReal root cause: fork(2) in a multi-threaded process.\n\nspawn_with_env forked and then, in the child before exec, called\nstd::env::vars / remove_var / set_var, std::env::set_current_dir, and built\nCStrings -- all of which allocate or take std's env lock. After fork the child\nhas only the calling thread but inherits every lock in whatever state it was in.\nA lock another thread held at that instant is held forever, so the child\ndeadlocks. POSIX allows only async-signal-safe calls between fork and exec.\n\nCaught in the act. The parent's test thread sat in do_wait (blocked in waitpid)\nwhile its forked child still had comm=pty::tests::tes -- it had never reached\nexec -- blocked in futex_do_wait on a lock nobody would release.\n\nIsolated reproduction, lib tests only:\n  default parallelism:  13/30 runs hung or failed\n  --test-threads=1:      0/30\n\nFix: build the environment, argv, envp and cwd as CStrings and NULL-terminated\npointer arrays BEFORE the fork; in the child do only close/setsid/ioctl/dup2/\nchdir, install the environment with a single pointer store to environ, then\nexecvp. No allocation, no std::env, after the fork.\n\n  after fix, same loop:  0/30\n\nThis was a production bug, not just a test problem: the server forks agents from\na thread pool, so 'vessel spawn' could hang the same way. That it surfaced in\ntests first is luck.\n\nAlso added the pty env tests that did not exist: explicit vars reach the child,\nunrelated parent vars do not leak, essential vars are inherited, explicit\noverrides essential, cwd applies, and a bare command still resolves via PATH."}	blake3:Z21jx_jSwHUcOlqEz12YwlMBtZQb9ztqfN0pX1ZiXwg
1785516066777458	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyQQ		item.create	bn-5fcn	{"description":"'cargo test --test cli test_spawn_duplicate_name_fails' fails every time when\nrun alone, on unmodified trunk. It passes in some full-suite runs, which is why\nit read as flaky rather than broken.\n\nFailure: the SECOND 'spawn --name unique-name' succeeds (code=0,\nstdout='unique-name') instead of being rejected with 'already in use'.\n\nDuplicate detection itself is fine -- it works correctly by hand against a\nmanually started server, rejecting the second spawn with 'agent name already in\nuse'. So this is about the test environment, not the name check.\n\nSuspect a race between TestEnv::start_server (which spawns 'vessel server' then\nsleeps a fixed 200ms) and the CLI auto-start path that most commands take when\nno server answers. If the explicit server has not finished binding, a spawn\nauto-starts its own; the stale-socket cleanup in Server::run can then remove the\nsocket entry out from under one of them, leaving the two spawns talking to\ndifferent servers -- so the second sees no existing name.\n\nEvidence for the race: the whole test completes in 0.23s despite the 200ms\nsleep, so the margin is thin.\n\nVerified identical on trunk and in the bn-20v1 workspace (12/12 failures in\nboth), so it is unrelated to the fork-safety fix in bn-20v1.\n\nFix direction: have start_server poll for readiness (ping until it answers)\ninstead of sleeping a fixed 200ms, and/or disable auto-start for these tests.\nWorth auditing the other tests that call start_server for the same assumption.","kind":"bug","title":"test_spawn_duplicate_name_fails fails 12/12 when run in isolation"}	blake3:0Vo-Usn-3b67blSRolJTywRQajJrGQaLo3V_qx-JU4c
1785516118919507	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyQg		item.comment	bn-20v1	{"body":"Fork-safety fix merged (9408d143). Verified on merged trunk: 0/20 lib-test\nruns failed, fmt and clippy clean.\n\nThe hangs are gone. One red test remains in the full suite,\ntest_spawn_duplicate_name_fails, which is a separate pre-existing defect --\nit fails 12/12 in isolation on unmodified trunk too. Filed as bn-5fcn.\n\nLeaving this bone open until bn-5fcn is resolved and a full 'cargo test\n--workspace' is green end to end."}	blake3:GCeg91WsLTDet5VAjSkOHbRxbkr7J68BteVMFqnY0Jg
1785516118934502	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyQw		item.link	bn-20v1	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-5fcn"}	blake3:M0vV-QAEjCORa4pBWUN_9Xi1spQ0JoIjkCGGpgoZwbA
