1785518950757064	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyRQ		item.assign	bn-3dsg	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:NnLjNVkl-MYUIKDAmceF8gW-f8NPgd-JXmgWgkp11Yk
1785520147166299	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyRg		item.comment	bn-5fcn	{"body":"Fixed by the asupersync 0.2.9 -> 0.3.10 upgrade (bn-3dsg), not by a test change.\n\n  0.2.9:  12/12 failures running the test in isolation\n  0.3.10:  0/12 failures\n\nSo my hypothesis in the description was wrong: it was not TestEnv::start_server\nracing the CLI auto-start. The second spawn really was reaching a different\nserver than the first, and the cause was in asupersync's Unix socket path, fixed\nupstream in 0.3.10."}	blake3:g19b7qY1QlCAyNiNUzU34yhuQXL08rgDAMySVukC0sY
1785520147180913	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyRw		item.move	bn-5fcn	{"state":"done"}	blake3:pOkGtK_XYN1fw0zB0HtnfshwgxnNg878lpuiRZQqJ08
1785520155709887	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEySA		item.comment	bn-20v1	{"body":"Resolved. The suite is green end to end.\n\nTwo independent causes, both now fixed:\n\n1. The hangs: fork(2) in a multi-threaded process, where the child called\n   allocating / lock-taking functions before exec. Fixed in this bone\n   (9408d143).\n\n2. test_spawn_duplicate_name_fails: an asupersync 0.2.9 socket bug, fixed by\n   the 0.3.10 upgrade in bn-3dsg. Tracked as bn-5fcn, now done.\n\nVerification on merged trunk (fefa823, asupersync 0.3.10):\n  cargo test --workspace   5/5 runs clean, 154 tests\n  lib-test loop            0/20 failures (was 13/30)\n  fmt + clippy             clean\n\nNote for whoever hits this next: the '--test-threads=4 makes it pass' signal in\nthe original report pointed at concurrency, which was right, but the mechanism\nwas fork-safety, not runtime worker counts. I chased the worker-count theory\nfirst and it was wrong -- see the correction comment above."}	blake3:_g8SDkzsj4ZpPrKs4337dMqqVdGf4H15YYIPQaau_F4
1785520155725911	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEySQ		item.move	bn-20v1	{"state":"done"}	blake3:tjofF63ZUJHmx_ghky8BzVGmVTuu87_mJdhDL4L62aY
1785520155741702	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEySg		item.move	bn-3dsg	{"state":"done"}	blake3:pw9KA4dn104ozvMNtohUY9WLW3Y2HeVeT9bUMtCfmjU
1785520167136366	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEySw		item.create	bn-1je9	{"description":"'cargo build --no-default-features --features otel,runtime-tokio' fails. This\npredates the asupersync 0.3.10 upgrade (bn-3dsg) -- verified on trunk before\nthat change -- so the tokio path has been broken for a while and is untested.\n\nError:\n\n  src/server/mod.rs:1627\n  () = poll_interval.tick() => { ... }\n  ^^ expected Instant, found ()\n\ntokio's Interval::tick() resolves to Instant; the asupersync shim's resolves to\n(). The select! arm pattern is written for the asupersync shape only. Two\noccurrences reported; there may be more behind them, since compilation stops at\nthe first batch.\n\nWhy it matters: Cargo.toml still advertises runtime-tokio as a supported\nfeature, and the two runtimes are mutually exclusive, so 'cargo clippy\n--workspace' and 'cargo test --workspace' only ever exercise the default\n(asupersync). Nothing in CI or the local gates would catch further rot.\n\nDecide which way to go:\n  a) Fix it and add a CI job that builds (ideally tests) the tokio feature, or\n  b) Drop runtime-tokio. runtime.rs describes it as a migration aid -- 'once\n     migration is complete, the tokio path is removed' -- and the migration\n     looks complete.\n\nOption (b) would delete a good deal of cfg-gated duplication in runtime.rs.\nWorth a decision either way rather than leaving a feature that does not build.","kind":"bug","title":"runtime-tokio backend does not compile"}	blake3:MzGrd4v_RZNizickTjQsxN7lsEis8yYK6NEN1d_6V1o
1785520392400097	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyTA		item.move	bn-1je9	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:X3wgHwe2Ltle65sbDDbUfe_PVzSAnuVOxs8kaFkT3mc
1785520392403889	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyTQ		item.assign	bn-1je9	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:8R1qXUkjo2RQQRrL5YAUg7zjK08NB20q-E1HwY8oXqs
1785521508145214	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyTg		item.move	bn-5fcn	{"reopen":true,"state":"open"}	blake3:sTNyoYeL3w-NPPZKZBeasoEvvmT9XVDec4EdAY-_hqE
1785521508160768	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyTw		item.comment	bn-5fcn	{"body":"Correction: I closed this too early. The 0.3.10 upgrade improved it a lot but\ndid not fully fix it.\n\nWhat I had when I closed it: 12/12 failures in isolation on 0.2.9, 0/12 on\n0.3.10, plus 4/4 clean full-suite runs. That was too thin.\n\nBetter numbers, 15 runs of the cli suite each:\n  trunk (0.3.10)                 3/15 failures\n  bn-1je9 branch (tokio removed) 0/15 failures\n  isolation, either              0/15 failures\n\nAnd a full-workspace loop on the bn-1je9 branch hit it once in 5.\n\nSo: the deterministic isolation failure is genuinely fixed by 0.3.10, and a\nresidual intermittent failure remains under full-suite load, roughly 20% of cli\nsuite runs on trunk. Removing the tokio backend did not cause it and if anything\nmeasures better, but the sample is too small to claim that as a real difference.\n\nStill the same symptom: the second 'spawn --name unique-name' succeeds instead\nof being rejected, so the two spawns are reaching different servers. The\nstart_server readiness fix from the original description is still the thing to\ntry -- poll ping until the server answers rather than sleeping a fixed 200ms."}	blake3:KH0SOQsf9yX33wuO8ULSHlQCbbEC9LotdWzniEWp-HI
1785521515983811	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyUA		item.comment	bn-20v1	{"body":"Amendment: bn-5fcn is reopened, so the 'green end to end' claim above is\nslightly too strong. The hangs this bone was about are genuinely fixed\n(0/20 lib loops, and no further hangs in any run since). What remains is an\nintermittent test_spawn_duplicate_name_fails, ~20% of cli-suite runs, tracked\nin bn-5fcn. Leaving this bone done since the fork deadlock is resolved."}	blake3:aWthN0rOP5J-4lLA3JCDbtX8w55q0yoasqyDkNU4j3A
1785521516000862	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyUQ		item.move	bn-1je9	{"state":"done"}	blake3:hOo1tUn3JBg5s99-9U3Fjzf_XB6UmAlKUz_n5iIHF8c
1785522599892796	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyUg		item.create	bn-16bx	{"description":"The 'otel' feature (default-on) wires OpenTelemetry OTLP export for traces and\nlogs. It was speculative and has never been used, and it is the single largest\ncontributor to the dependency tree: it pulls opentelemetry, opentelemetry_sdk,\nopentelemetry-otlp, opentelemetry-appender-tracing, tracing-opentelemetry, and\nthrough opentelemetry-otlp's reqwest-blocking client it drags in reqwest, hyper,\ntonic and tokio.\n\nRemoving it is the real dependency win that the runtime-tokio removal (bn-1je9)\nwas not -- tokio only stays in the graph because otel pulls it.\n\nNothing about it is documented: no mention of OTEL, opentelemetry, or TRACEPARENT\nin README.md, docs/ or AGENTS.md. It is entirely internal surface.\n\nTo remove:\n  - Cargo.toml: the 'otel' feature and its five optional deps\n  - telemetry.rs: init_otlp, install_parent_context, the otel arm of init(),\n    the otel fields on TelemetryGuard and its Drop, and current_traceparent\n  - server/mod.rs: the TRACEPARENT auto-injection into spawned agent env, which\n    is dead without otel (current_traceparent reads the active span context,\n    which only exists under otel)\n\nTo keep:\n  - tracing / tracing-subscriber and all the instrument/info/warn/debug calls:\n    that is vessel's actual logging, unrelated to otel\n  - init_fmt, including VESSEL_LOG file logging\n  - the JSON-to-stderr formatter, which is tracing-subscriber only and needs no\n    otel deps -- but it is currently triggered by\n    OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=stderr, which makes no sense once otel is gone,\n    so retrigger it from a vessel-native variable.","kind":"task","title":"Remove the otel/OTLP telemetry export"}	blake3:dc45jqjygH31vLvZvN7wElfFQZWW_squADk535XPnfE
1785522606826779	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyUw		item.move	bn-16bx	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:dQcj1R6gZ-Ew-K9gRwgkbMD42Q1gN-Py0NhANQDAGrc
1785522606830684	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyVA		item.assign	bn-16bx	{"action":"assign","agent":"vessel-dev"}	blake3:VEh--qaDwUAVvW0P90ZGSQkdWzV1m2uT6ZdVtdnyijU
1785523951219211	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyVQ		item.comment	bn-16bx	{"body":"Merged (d4e5ba28). Measured on trunk after merge:\n\n  unique crates   245 -> 177\n  debug binary    446 MB -> 401 MB\n  telemetry.rs    282 -> 113 lines\n  cold build      ~80s both ways (no change; 64 cores means the critical path\n                  dominates, not crate count -- may differ on a small runner)\n\nGone with it: tokio, reqwest, hyper, tonic, mio, rand, url/idna and the ICU\nchain. Cargo.toml no longer has a [features] table.\n\nOne user-visible change: JSON logging moved from\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=stderr to VESSEL_LOG_FORMAT=json. Both it and\nVESSEL_LOG are now documented in the README; neither was before.\n\nVerified: fmt + clippy clean, 5/5 clean suite runs on the branch and 3/3 on\nmerged trunk, both logging modes exercised by hand against the built binary."}	blake3:kdyZFp5fW5NxgVvWwJmu1a5cBQbifjCd_VnFMqPkyyI
1785523951237038	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyVg		item.move	bn-16bx	{"state":"done"}	blake3:u54TEEEnHeDycCjdUOYsqA8CUJOp6HtSPpPHNHzlHog
